# Copy to `.env` only if you need to override the default.
#
# Leaving DATABASE_URL unset is fine for local development: the app falls back to
# a SQLite file at prisma/dev.db (see src/server/db/database-url.ts).
#
# SQLite (default):
# DATABASE_URL="file:./prisma/dev.db"
#
# Postgres (see docs/backend.md before switching):
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://soko:soko@localhost:5432/soko?schema=public"

# --- Firebase (client) ---
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in from the Firebase console:
#   Project settings -> Your apps -> SDK setup and configuration.
#
# These are public identifiers, not secrets. A Firebase web API key is served to
# every visitor in the client bundle by design; Google documents it as safe to
# expose. What protects the project is Security Rules and App Check — never
# treat these as credentials, and never assume a locked-down repo is protection.
#
# All are required except MEASUREMENT_ID, without which Analytics is skipped.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=""

# --- Firebase App Check (client) ---
#
# The reCAPTCHA v3 *site* key, from Firebase console -> App Check -> Apps.
# Public for the same reason as the keys above: a site key belongs in the page,
# and the secret half never leaves Google. Leave empty and App Check is simply
# not initialised — the site works, it is just unattested.
#
# Enforcement is a separate switch, per product, in the console. Turn it on in
# monitor-only mode first and read the metrics before enforcing, or you will
# lock out real users you cannot see.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_CHECK_SITE_KEY=""

# A debug token registered under App Check -> Apps -> Manage debug tokens, for
# localhost and CI where reCAPTCHA cannot score a real browser. Per-developer
# and revocable. It belongs in .env.local ONLY — a debug token in the repo lets
# anyone mint valid App Check tokens, which is the one thing App Check exists to
# prevent.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_CHECK_DEBUG_TOKEN=""

# --- Firebase Admin (server) — THESE ARE REAL SECRETS ---
#
# Everything above this line is a public identifier. Everything below it is not,
# and the difference is not a matter of degree: a service-account key is a
# private key granting full project access, and it bypasses both Security Rules
# and App Check by design. It must never be committed, never be given a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix (which would ship it to every visitor), and never be
# pasted into a chat, an issue or a pull request.
#
# Set ONE of the two below. With neither set, sign-in is unavailable and the
# site degrades to exactly its previous behaviour: order requests are built in
# the browser and held in sessionStorage. That is a supported configuration.
#
# 1. The contents of the JSON key as a single line — what a deployment secret
#    store can actually hold. Firebase console -> Project settings ->
#    Service accounts -> Generate new private key.
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY='{"type":"service_account","project_id":"...","private_key":"-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...","client_email":"..."}'
#
# 2. Or a filesystem path to that same file, Google's documented form. The path
#    must point OUTSIDE this repository.
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS="/absolute/path/outside/this/repo/service-account.json"
#
# On Cloud Run, App Engine or Cloud Functions neither is needed: the runtime
# supplies Application Default Credentials itself.
#
# Optional. Only needed if the Admin SDK should act against a different project
# from the one in NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID.
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=""
